Q: How Best to Dynamically Link Available Files during Compile Time A: <blockquote>
<p>but I don't want to constantly have to update the main program whenever I add an interpreter for a new filetype.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Create a library with only one simple interface:</p>
<pre><code>//e.g. library.h
image* loadImage(const char *filename /*, optional: const char *filetype */);
</code></pre>
<p>then your main program only needs to link to one library and include a single header file:</p>
<pre><code>//e.g. main.c
#include &quot;library.h&quot;

int main()
{
    image *img = loadImage(&quot;some_image_file&quot; /*, optional: &quot;png&quot; */);
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you update the library (e.g., add a new interpreter), you only need to distribute the <strong>library</strong>, not the <strong>program</strong>. Of course, the library's source file would then need to contain all the interpreter headers; I'm not sure if this approach is suitable.</p>
<pre><code>//so, instead in main.c, you would have to do in library.c
#include &quot;bmp.h&quot;
#include &quot;png.h&quot;
//...

image* loadImage(const char *filename /*, optional: const char *filetype */)
{
    //depending on the file extension or filetype parameter,
    //invoke the corresponding function to load the image
}
</code></pre>
<p>For the sake of completeness, it should be mentioned that you would need to create an object file for each interpreter and then link them to generate the library. My suggestion is to shift the problem from the program to the library. This approach allows you to add or remove different interpreters without having to change the code in other programs that use this library (the principle of shared libraries). If you want to automate the compilation and linking process, you'll find better/other suggestions in the comments above or in @dbush's answer.</p>

Q: How to add related aggregate data in Laravel Eloquent? A: <p>I had hoped to use Laravel's own eager loading but with a derived 'model' from my stats query, but I don't think this is possible; Laravel's eager loading is too tied to Eloquent models. However, I did find this solution:</p>

<h2>Changes to the Metric Model</h2>

<p>We will:</p>

<ol>
<li><p>Create a measurementStats attribute (a very long winded way of making a public property!)</p></li>
<li><p>Tell the model to use a custom collection instead of the normal Eloquent collection.</p></li>
<li><p>Tell the model to append the stats attribute when turning itself into JSON.</p></li>
</ol>

<p>App/Metric.php:</p>

<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>namespace App;

use App\MetricCollection;
...
class Metric
{
  ...

  protected $appends = ['measurementStats'];

  /** @var array */
  protected $measurementStats;

  public function getMeasurementStatsAttribute() {
    return $this-&gt;measurementStats ?? [];
  }

  public function setMeasurementStatsAttribute($stats) {
    $this-&gt;measurementStats = $stats;
  }

  public function newCollection(array $models=[]) {
    return new MetricCollection($models);
  }
</code></pre>

<h2>Custom collection</h2>

<p>This will enable us to load all the measurement stats in one query and attach them to the models.</p>

<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>namespace App;

  use \Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Collection;
  use \DB;

  class MetricCollection extends Collection
  {
    public function addMeasurementStats($filters) {
      $metric_ids = $this-&gt;modelKeys();
      $stats_query = DB::table('measurements')
        -&gt;select('metric_id')
        -&gt;selectRaw("DATE_FORMAT(timestamp, '%Y%m%d') as period")
        -&gt;selectRaw("MIN(value) AS min")
        -&gt;selectRaw("MAX(value) AS max")
        -&gt;selectRaw("AVG(value) AS value")
        -&gt;whereIn('metric_id', $metric_ids)
        -&gt;groupBy('metric_id', 'period');

      if (!empty($filters['since'])) {
        $stats_query-&gt;where('timestamp', '&gt;=', $filters['since']);
      }
      foreach ($stats_query-&gt;get()-&gt;groupBy('metric_id') as $metric_id =&gt; $stats) {
        $this-&gt;find($metric_id)-&gt;measurementStats = $stats;
      }
      return $this;
    }
  }
</code></pre>

<h2>Finally, fetch the metrics with stats</h2>

<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>$data = Metric::all()-&gt;addMeasrementStats(['since' =&gt; '2019-07-31']);
</code></pre>

Q: Edit and Updating Data in Laravel A: <p>About the error:</p>

<p>In your view, you've got this piece of code</p>

<pre><code>{{ Form::open(array('route' =&gt; array('edit_spk', 'id'=&gt; $spk_data-&gt;id), 'method' =&gt; 'GET', 'style' =&gt; 'display:inline')) }}
</code></pre>

<p>the <code>$spk</code> variable is an eloquent collection, that is returned on your controller. You're accessing $spk->id, which is incorrect, need to access the a single element in the collection in order to edit it</p>

<p>Allow me to simplify your code in the meanwhile:</p>

<p>In your route, you specify what method in the controller will handle the request.
Write in your console:</p>

<pre><code>php artisan make:request SpkContent
</code></pre>

<p><strong>SpkController</strong></p>

<pre><code>use App\Http\Requests\SpkContent;

public function edit($id, SpkContent $request){

  $spk = Spk::find($id);
  //Not sure what this is for really, but you can simplify the lines below with this one
  //$data = $request-&gt;validated();
  //$spk = array_merge($spk-&gt;toArray(), $data);
  $spk-&gt;distribution_code=Input::get('distribution_code');
  $spk-&gt;destination=Input::get('destination');
  $spk-&gt;hlr=Input::get('hlr');
  $spk-&gt;first_iccid=Input::get('first_iccid');
  $spk-&gt;last_iccid=Input::get('last_iccid');
  $spk-&gt;quantity=Input::get('quantity');
  return View::make('modals.edit-spk', compact('spk'));
}

public function update($id, SpkContent $request) {
    // store
    $data = $request-&gt;validated();
    Spk::findOrFail($id)-&gt;update($data);

    // redirect
    Session::flash('message', 'Successfully updated SPK !');
    return Redirect::to('spk_view');
}
</code></pre>

<p><strong>SpkContent FormRequest</strong></p>

<pre><code>class SpkContent extends FormRequest
{
    public function authorize()
    {
        return true;
    }

    public function rules()
    {
        //Add whatever validations you need, as in: max size, integers, etc
        $content = [
         'distribution_code' =&gt; 'required',
         'destination' =&gt; 'required',
         'hlr'=&gt; 'required',
         'first_iccid' =&gt; 'required',
         'last_iccid' =&gt; 'required',
         'quantity' =&gt; 'required'
        ];
        return $content;
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>At least, this way you can split validation from operation, much cleaner and maintainable code</p>
